column1:
1:9|Her uncleanness is on her skirts;
she gave no thought to her end.
Her fall was astonishing;
no one comforts her.
“Look, **LORD**, at my affliction,
for the enemy has made himself great!”|Marks the first-person interruption and preserves the recurring absent comforter.

1:20|Look, **LORD**, how distressed I am!
My insides churn;
my heart turns over within me,
for I have rebelled deeply.
Outside, the sword bereaves;
inside, it is like death.|Keeps the bodily turmoil and the source's qualified like-death phrase.

2:6|He tore away his shelter as if it were a garden;
he destroyed his appointed meeting place.
The **LORD** made feast and Sabbath forgotten in Zion;
in his fierce anger he spurned king and priest.|Preserves the difficult garden comparison and the distinct divine name.

2:9|Her gates sank into the ground;
he destroyed and broke their bars.
Her king and officials are among the nations.
There is no instruction;
even her prophets find
no vision from the **LORD**.|Keeps the loss of civic, instructional, and prophetic resources distinct.

4:16|The **LORD**'s presence scattered them;
he will look on them no longer.
Priests received no honor;
elders received no favor.|Retains the divine face/presence sense and parallels rejected leaders.

4:22|Your punishment is complete, daughter Zion;
he will not send you into exile again.
But he will call your guilt to account, daughter Edom;
he will expose your sins.|Retains contrasting futures and guilt/punishment range rather than harmonizing with the unresolved ending.
